the Reform  Party was entitled to 58 million dollars  worth of matching funds at the time  something they're no longer entitled to  because of the strong showings of Ross  Perot in 92 and 96 so you had the  prospect of running for president on OPM  other people's money yeah that appealed  to trump the businessman as you might  expect but he correctly concluded after  a really intense exploration that the  country is not going to elect an  independent president anytime soon that  just ballot access is controlled by the  Republicans and the Democrats working  together and therefore one really has to  be a Republican or a Democrat to win the  election as president he switched back  to the party of his parents and the  party that he spent most of his adult  life  the Republican Party talked about a  little bit of the tensions within the  campaign this was dramatic moments early  in the campaign between Paul Manafort  who was the face to campaign person who  came in and Corey Lewandowski he was the  phase one person who was there  orchestrating the big campaign rallies  that really launched Trump into the  stratosphere as a candidate and how did  man afford how was he able to transform  as what you described sort of an unruly  campaign into something that had a lot  more discipline and and how do you carry  that out so quickly  and do you think it was you know  successful in your estimation and he did  win but there were certainly stumbles  along the way there in the second part  of the campaign well Corey Lewandowski  is an advanced man mm-hmm  and for that period of the campaign  that's what was called for Paul Manafort  was able to call on a deep bench of  political associates going back through  many presidential campaigns to lend a  hand people with more experience at the  state level he was able to pull the very  best out of the existing Trump  organization to whip together an  organization going into the convention  to surmount the challenges there it's a  substantial task but the successful  nomination of Trump quashing the Ted  Cruz forces in the Rules Committee in  their attempt to try to hijack the  nomination demonstrated that he was  successful also I think though the  convention has to be viewed as what it  really is a television show mm-hmm it's  about entertainment and Paul Manafort  helped Donald Trump stage a very  successful convention the highlighting  of the Trump children the terrific  speech to rally the faithful by Rudy  Giuliani general Flynn's speech there  are a lot of high points in this  convention we have a larger audience for  our convention than the Democrats  unheard of and they pulled it out they  pulled it together very quickly without  much structure so me and afford I think  and deserves and gets a lot of credit in  the book that's around the time that you  departed the campaign can you talk about  that a little bit and what led to your  leaving well I left in August because my  book on the Clintons was coming out and  I didn't want Trump to be accused of  being the secret you know progenitor of  that because he wasn't because I had a  fundamental disagreement on campaign  strategy at the time I mean I really  think I've already addressed it I think  he needed to run a more traditional  campaign and I did think that some of  the Twitter fights that he got in  distracted from his overall message of  immigration and tax reform and and trade  and so on again he was right and I was  wrong about a lot of it but I also  appointed myself as the number one  surrogate for Donald Trump I probably  didn't five six hundred interviews or  surrogate speeches between the time I  left an election day because I felt I  knew the man for almost 40 years and I  could tell people firsthand why he would  be a great president why I was forum  about his independence about his  resilience about his toughness so I  never stopped being a Donald Trump  supporter but at 64 years old I wasn't  going to be in a campaign structure in  which I had to approve what I was going  to write or say mm-hmm I felt I could be  of greater help from the outside and I  think I was during the convention there  was a dramatic moment when senator Cruz  did not endorse Trump on the stage he  just told the voters delegates to vote  their instinct or their conscience  instead of voting for Trump what was his  response to that and was that a surprise  to you I think it was a surprise to  everyone in the campaign because Paul  Manafort had made it clear to senator  Cruz and to Jeff Roe his manager that he  didn't have to use the word endorse but  he had to say that he was supporting  Donald Trump and the ticket this was I  think one of the great miss readings of  politics if senator Cruz thought that  Trump was going to lose and he clearly  did  the smart thing to do would have been to  do what Richard Nixon did in 1964 when  he was certain that Goldwater was loose  would lose give him a full-throated  endorsement and campaigned for him every  place that way if Trump lost people  would say good old Ted Cruz man he was  really there for the party he broke his  back for the ticket  he gets consideration next time instead  Ted Cruz decided to be Nelson  Rockefeller take his ball and go home  hardcore conservatives and Trump  supporters will never let him be the  nominee of the party because he turned  his back on the party when the prospects  didn't look good now later on when he  figures out that Trump is likely to win  he endorses Trump too little too late  senator so I think he made an enormous  mistake I guess he thought that the  quality of his rhetoric would be so  great that it would be like the 1976  moment when Ronald Reagan addressed the  convention after Ford's nomination and  it was true many people in that  convention hall said my god we have  nominated the wrong man he's not Ronald  Reagan in fact he's more like Richard  Nixon than Ronald Reagan what was  Trump's response was he surprised by  that hurt by it angry did he have any  wish I think he I think he was unhappy  but he was later on I think quite  generous towards the senator and I think  he accepted his later endorsement with  good grace too had gone back and forth  and traded insults there was Trump's  declaration that Ted Cruz's father had  been in conversations with the Kennedy  assassin and you write a little bit  about that in your book what do you make  of that it some people would say well  why are we talking about this it's not  relevant so but it you make it part of  your book can you talk about that a  little bit sure I believe that that  Senator Cruz's father did in fact know  Lee Lee Harvey Oswald Judith very Baker  who is indisputably an associate of  Oswald writes this in her book she said  it in multi  we'll interviews the photograph you seen  comes from the Warren Commission not  from the National Enquirer I think  Donald Trump threw it out there to get  under Ted Cruz's skin and make him erupt  that was not fair that was patriotism  when in fact no questioning his  patriotism was questioning his  temperament interesting look the  question is obvious why do people take  an instantaneous dislike to Ted Cruz  they're merely saving time so you feel  like Trump's tactics of trying to get an  emotional rise out of people on Twitter  should he do that now in the White House  or should he back off on that only for a  strategic purpose in this case he got  Ted Cruz so angry who was drooling you  can see him getting into a fight with a  ten-year-old boy in a rally so and the  kid keeps yelling lyin Ted lyin Ted  buttocks in a way you could argue  backfire because Cruz cited that as well  as I think there was some disparaging  things said about his wife Heidi as  reasons why he was not able to endorse  Trump and we could argue perhaps the  Cruz endorsement might have helped Trump  at the convention Trump won therefore I  think senator Cruz is the loser and his  entire calculation look he is a bush  Republican masquerading as something  else and and I call him out very  extensively in the book you think he'll  survive a 28 trying to run again in 2018  I would hope that somebody will  challenge him in the Republican primary  in Texas maybe the maybe the Attorney  General if not perhaps Alex Jones will  run who knows but somebody needs to  challenge him but if he survives that  and he has latent plans to run for  president I think many Republicans are  going to remember the fact that he  wouldn't endorse the ticket at the  convention you talk about some of your  favorite or most relevant passages in  the book that you find are you know work  really important to you that you want  people to take notice of sure Harry hurt  in his biography of Donald Trump and the  late Wayne Barrett who just passed away  recently the village  I've both written that a story that  circulated that indicated that Donald  Trump was scheduled to fly to Atlantic  City from New York and a helicopter that  would tragically crash in the Pinelands  and kill everybody aboard that that was  made up that Donald Trump never intended  to be on that helicopter that there was  no plans but that he promulgated the  story to generate favorable publicity I  can tell you firsthand that they're  wrong  BuzzFeed Harry hurt Wayne Barrett  because I was working for Donald Trump  as a lobbyist working on currency  transaction issues I was in Washington  he was in New York I called him and said  I needed to see him as soon as possible  to go over some compromise language that  needed an approval immediately and he  said I can't see you today I've got to  go to Atlantic City I said I could jump  the shuttle and be there in an hour and  a half it's really important he said all  right I'll send the helicopter ahead and  hard to come back and pick me up I was  sitting in his office when Norma Federer  his longtime assistant came in and told  him that the superintendent of state  police in New Jersey Clint Pagano was on  the phone he put the veteran cop on the  speakerphone and he said mr. Trump I  have terrible news the helicopter your  company chartered has crashed and there  are no survivors and Donald said are you  absolutely sure and Pagano said yes a  hundred percent and of course women were  crying there was a horrific time to his  credit Donald called every one of the  widows right then and there in some  cases breaking the news to them which I  could never have done so Donald Trump  was telling the truth now I am not  claiming that I saved his life  I really believe that this was an act of  divine intervention that this is bigger  than than any one person and at that  point I decided that God had spared  Trump for a purpose some future purpose  we know what that know what that purpose  is  very dramatic but Trump was telling the  truth about the entire incident and he  was smeared by his critics what a story  Roger stone thanks for being here to  make a to the president 2016 now  available delighted to be here thanks
 

